# Line endings.
#
# Everything is normalised to LF in the repository, and most files are checked out with
# whatever the platform prefers. These are not most files.
#
# The scripts and unit files below are written on Windows and executed on Linux. With
# core.autocrlf=true — the Git for Windows default — a fresh clone writes CRLF into the
# working tree, the packager copies that into the bundle, and the Pi ends up with a
# shebang reading `#!/bin/sh<CR>`. The kernel looks for an interpreter with a carriage
# return in its name and reports "no such file or directory", naming a file that plainly
# exists. systemd unit files fail the same way, less loudly.
#
# This does not show up on the machine that wrote the files, because their working copy
# already has LF. It shows up for the next person to clone, which is worse.
* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf

# The Windows service helpers are the mirror image: they run on the machine they are
# checked out to, and cmd.exe wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
*.tar.gz binary
